    "articleTitle": "The Night I Stopped Ordering Takeaway",
    "articleEn": "Last year I used to {{order}} a {{takeaway}} almost every night, and my diet slowly got worse. Then a friend who works as a {{chef}} invited me to his flat and changed my mind about home cooking. He is not a {{gourmet}} snob; he simply believes that simple food, cooked well, tastes better than anything delivered in a plastic box.\n\nHe is also {{vegetarian}}, so we started with vegetables. While he heated oil in a heavy {{pan}}, he asked me to lay out the {{cutlery}}. His grandmother's {{silver}} spoons sat beside cheap {{ceramic}} plates and one thin {{porcelain}} cup, and somehow the mixture looked lovely. I filled a wooden {{bowl}} with salad, carried the main {{dish}} to the table, and put a small {{saucer}} under each cup. Everything else went onto a metal {{tray}}: a {{fork}} and {{knife}} for each of us, a wide {{spoon}} for the soup, a {{glass}} of water, and my favourite blue {{mug}} for tea. Later he boiled the {{kettle}} twice, because we sat talking for hours.\n\nThat evening cost less than two takeaways and left me feeling light instead of heavy. Now I cook three or four times a week. I still order food sometimes, but it is a treat, not a habit. Good health, I have learned, often begins with the ordinary objects in your own kitchen.",
    "articleZh": "去年我几乎每天晚上都要点外卖，饮食也慢慢变差了。后来一位当厨师的朋友请我去他的公寓，改变了我对在家做饭的看法。他并不是个讲究吃的美食家式的挑剔者；他只是相信简单的食物只要做得好，就比任何装在塑料盒里送来的东西都好吃。\n\n他还是素食者，所以我们从蔬菜开始。他在一只厚重的平底锅里热油时，让我去摆餐具。他祖母的银勺子摆在便宜的陶瓷盘子和一只薄薄的瓷杯旁边，这样的搭配看起来竟然很好看。我在一个木碗里装满沙拉，把主菜端上桌，又在每个杯子下面放了一个小茶碟。其他东西都放在一个金属托盘上：我们每人一副叉子和刀，一把喝汤用的大勺子，一杯水，还有我最喜欢的那只蓝色马克杯用来喝茶。后来他把水壶烧开了两次，因为我们坐着聊了好几个小时。\n\n那一晚的花费还不到两份外卖的钱，却让我觉得身体轻松，而不是沉重。现在我每周做三四次饭。我偶尔还是会点餐，但那是一种享受，不再是习惯。我明白了：良好的健康，往往就从你自家厨房里那些普通的物件开始。",
