    "id": "c12p3",
    "chapter": 12,
    "page": 3,
    "articleTitle": "Health Begins in the Kitchen",
    "articleEn": "My grandmother believes health begins in the kitchen. Every evening she puts a heavy {{pot}} on the {{stove}} and lets soup simmer for an hour. The room feels as warm as a {{furnace}}, especially when the {{oven}} is on too, roasting fish and garlic. She almost never opens a {{tin}} of processed meat; instead she lifts the {{lid}} of her pot and adds fresh {{vegetables}} such as carrots, cabbage and beans.\n\nShe also has firm rules about what we {{drink}}. Any sweet {{beverage}} is limited: a small glass of orange {{juice}} at breakfast is fine, but {{soda}} appears only at birthdays, because the sugar is hidden and the calories are empty. She allows one cup of {{coffee}} in the morning and none after four o'clock, so that sleep stays deep.\n\n{{Alcohol}} is the topic she cares about most. Strong {{liquor}}, including {{whisky}} and {{brandy}}, damages the liver quietly, she says, and people who get {{drunk}} every weekend usually eat badly as well. She feels the same about {{tobacco}}: one {{cigarette}} may seem harmless, but the habit grows fast. Guests are welcome to stay {{sober}} at her table, and nobody laughs at them for it.\n\nIs she extreme? Perhaps a little. Yet at seventy-eight she still walks to the market, chooses her own food and cooks it herself. Her advice is simple: eat what your body recognises, and look after your kitchen better than your medicine cabinet.",
    "articleZh": "我奶奶相信,健康是从厨房开始的。每天傍晚,她都会把一口沉重的锅放在炉灶上,让汤慢炖一个小时。屋里热得像个火炉,尤其是当烤箱也开着、正烤着鱼和蒜的时候。她几乎从不打开一罐加工肉类;她只是揭开锅盖,加入新鲜的蔬菜,比如胡萝卜、白菜和豆子。\n\n她对我们喝什么也有严格的规定。任何甜味饮料都要限量:早餐喝一小杯橙汁没问题,但汽水只在生日时才出现,因为其中的糖分很隐蔽,热量却毫无营养。她允许早上喝一杯咖啡,四点以后就不许再喝,这样睡眠才能踏实。\n\n酒精是她最在意的话题。她说,烈酒——包括威士忌和白兰地——会悄无声息地损害肝脏,而那些每个周末都喝醉的人,通常吃得也很糟糕。她对烟草的看法也一样:一支香烟看起来似乎无害,但这个习惯会迅速养成。客人在她的餐桌上完全可以保持清醒不喝酒,没有人会因此笑话他们。\n\n她是不是太极端了?也许有一点。但七十八岁的她仍然自己走去菜市场,自己挑选食材,自己下厨。她的建议很简单:吃你的身体认得出来的东西,并且照顾厨房要比照顾药箱更用心。",
