    "id": "c12p4",
    "chapter": 12,
    "page": 4,
    "articleTitle": "A Basket of Colours",
    "articleEn": "My grandmother believes that health begins in the kitchen, not in a pharmacy. Every Saturday we walk to the market with an empty basket and come home with a rainbow. She always buys a red {{tomato}} or two, a heavy {{potato}} for soup, and a small bag of green {{pea}}s that we shell together at the table. She adds a handful of white {{bean}}s, a cool {{cucumber}} for salad, half a {{cabbage}}, one strong {{onion}}, and a few brown {{mushroom}}s. If the price is fair, she also takes a purple {{eggplant}}, a sweet {{carrot}}, a pale {{turnip}}, and a spicy {{radish}}.\n\nAt home the real work starts. I {{peel}} the carrots and cut them into thin {{strip}}s, while she shows me how to {{hull}} strawberries quickly with a small knife. Fruit is never forgotten. There is usually a bowl of dark {{cherry}}, some wild {{berry}} from the hills, a bunch of green {{grape}}, a slice of soft {{papaya}}, and one perfect {{peach}} saved for my grandfather.\n\nNothing in her kitchen is fried, and nothing is wasted. She says a balanced plate should look like a garden: many colours, little salt, and no hurry. After twenty years of eating this way, she still climbs the stairs faster than I do, which is the most convincing argument I have ever heard.",
    "articleZh": "我的祖母相信，健康始于厨房，而不是药房。每个周六，我们提着空篮子走去市场，回家时带回一道彩虹。她总会买一两个红{{番茄}}、一个炖汤用的大{{土豆}}，还有一小袋绿{{豌豆}}，我们会一起坐在桌边剥壳。她还会加上一把白{{豆子}}、一根清爽的{{黄瓜}}做沙拉、半个{{卷心菜}}、一个味道浓烈的{{洋葱}}和几朵褐色{{蘑菇}}。如果价格合适，她也会挑一个紫色的{{茄子}}、一根甜{{胡萝卜}}、一个颜色浅淡的{{芜菁}}和一个辛辣的{{萝卜}}。\n\n回到家，真正的活儿才开始。我给胡萝卜{{削皮}}，把它们切成细{{条}}，她则教我怎样用小刀快速地给草莓{{去蒂}}。水果从不会被忘记。通常会有一碗深色的{{樱桃}}、一些山上采来的野{{浆果}}、一串青{{葡萄}}、一片软软的{{木瓜}}，还有一个留给我祖父的完美{{桃子}}。\n\n她的厨房里没有任何油炸的东西，也没有任何浪费。她说，一份均衡的餐盘应该像一座花园：颜色丰富、盐分很少、吃得不急。这样吃了二十年之后，她上楼梯依然比我快，这是我听过的最有说服力的论证。",
