    "id": "c14p6",
    "chapter": 14,
    "page": 6,
    "articleTitle": "The Long Way North by Rail",
    "articleEn": "Last winter I travelled across the country by {{rail}}. In Britain people say {{railway}}; in America they say {{railroad}}; the shining lines are the same. Near the station, a museum kept an old steam {{locomotive}}, polished and silent. A sign explained that city travellers now prefer the {{subway}} — Londoners call it the {{underground}} — because it slides beneath the traffic through a long {{tunnel}}.\n\nMy train was {{due}} to {{depart}} at nine, but a signal fault caused a two-hour {{delay}}. The staff chose to {{postpone}} the service rather than cancel it, so I had to {{defer}} my hotel booking and hope my cheap ticket would not {{expire}} before we finally {{arrived}}.\n\nDuring a quiet {{lull}} in the waiting hall, I bought a {{stamp}}, paid a little extra {{postage}}, and sent a small {{packet}} of photographs to my sister. The clerk promised that the {{mail}} van would leave at noon.\n\nWe rolled out at eleven. Snow covered the fields, and I stopped watching the clock. Travelling slowly, I decided, is not wasted time; it is thinking time. When we reached my {{destination}} that evening, my sister was waiting on the platform with hot tea, and the long day suddenly felt short.",
    "articleZh": "去年冬天，我乘火车横穿了这个国家。英国人说 railway，美国人说 railroad，但那些闪亮的铁轨是一样的。车站附近有一座博物馆，里面保存着一台老式蒸汽机车，擦得锃亮，安静地停在那里。一块牌子上写道，如今城里的人更喜欢坐地铁——伦敦人把它叫作 underground——因为它能穿过一条长长的隧道，从车流下面滑行而过。\n\n我的列车原定九点发车，但一处信号故障造成了两个小时的延误。工作人员决定推迟这趟车，而不是取消它，于是我只好把酒店预订延后，同时希望我那张便宜的车票不会在我们终于到达之前过期。\n\n候车厅里有一阵安静的间歇，我趁机买了一张邮票，多付了一点邮资，把一小包照片寄给了姐姐。柜台的职员保证，邮车中午就会出发。\n\n我们十一点才驶出车站。雪覆盖着田野，我也不再盯着时钟看了。我想，慢慢地旅行并不是浪费时间，而是用来思考的时间。那天傍晚，当我们抵达我的目的地时，姐姐已经端着热茶在站台上等我，漫长的一天忽然显得很短。",
