    "id": "c16p1",
    "chapter": 16,
    "page": 1,
    "articleTitle": "The Street Where Money Moves",
    "articleEn": "Every town has its own small {{economy}}, and mine is a good example. Two hundred years ago, local {{trade}} depended on a single {{market}} beside the river, where farmers sold whatever the fishing {{industry}} could not use. One {{merchant}} named Alice Grey grew rich there. When her {{financial}} situation improved, she did not keep everything for herself. She set up a {{fund}} to help poor families, chose to {{donate}} hundreds of books to the town school, and even {{endowed}} a scholarship that still exists today.\n\nThe same street is now completely {{commercial}}. Shops {{advertise}} loudly, and the old bakery has painted a cheerful {{slogan}} above its door: \"Bread for everyone.\" Young couples come here to {{purchase}} furniture, or to {{lease}} a small flat above the shops because they cannot {{afford}} to buy one. {{Retail}} space is expensive, so the {{grocery}} store on the corner survives by offering a weekly {{discount}} on vegetables and by running a summer {{promotion}} in which every customer receives a {{voucher}} for a free coffee.\n\nNothing on this street is dramatic, yet it shows clearly how money moves through a community: people earn, spend, save, and give. A healthy local economy is not only about profit and busy shops. It also depends on neighbours who decide, as Alice Grey once did, to put something back into the place that made them successful.",
    "articleZh": "每个城镇都有自己的小型经济体，我住的镇子就是个很好的例子。两百年前，当地的贸易依赖河边一个小市场，农民在那里出售渔业用不上的东西。一位名叫爱丽丝·格雷的商人在那里发了财。当她的财务状况好转后，她并没有把一切都留给自己。她设立了一个基金来帮助贫困家庭，选择向镇上的学校捐赠了几百本书，甚至还捐资设立了一项至今仍在发放的奖学金。\n\n如今，同一条街已完全商业化。店铺大声做广告，那家老面包店在门上刷了一句欢快的标语：“面包，人人有份。”年轻的夫妇来这里购买家具，或者租下店铺楼上的小公寓，因为他们买不起房子。零售铺面租金昂贵，所以街角那家杂货店靠每周给蔬菜打折，以及在夏天开展促销活动来维持生意——每位顾客都能领到一张免费咖啡的优惠券。\n\n这条街上没有什么惊天动地的事，但它清楚地展示了金钱如何在一个社区里流动：人们挣钱、花钱、存钱，也捐钱。一个健康的地方经济不只关乎利润和热闹的店铺，它还取决于那些像爱丽丝·格雷当年那样，愿意回馈这片让自己获得成功的土地的邻居们。",
