    "id": "c16p2",
    "chapter": 16,
    "page": 2,
    "articleTitle": "Money in Hard Times",
    "articleEn": "When prices rise quickly, ordinary shoppers change their habits. My neighbour Lina saves a {{coupon}} for every supermarket she visits, and she checks the {{currency}} rate before buying anything imported. At the weekend market she will {{exchange}} a bag of apples for fresh eggs, or {{swap}} old clothes with friends instead of buying new ones. She always asks for a {{receipt}}, and for her tiny online shop she files every {{invoice}} carefully, because the {{tax}} office may ask for proof.\n\nGovernments react too. They may {{levy}} an extra charge on sugary drinks, raise the {{tariff}} on imported cars, or collect a {{duty}} at the border. When officials {{impose}} rules like these, families {{consume}} less and {{spend}} more slowly. The {{cost}} of living climbs, everyday {{expenses}} grow, and company {{revenue}} falls. If this continues, the {{gross}} value of everything a country produces stops growing.\n\nEconomists then start using serious words. A {{recession}} means the economy shrinks for six months or more. A {{depression}} is far worse: years of weak demand, closed factories and high unemployment. Both often begin with a banking {{crisis}}, when lenders suddenly lose confidence and stop offering loans.\n\nStill, ordinary choices matter. Lina says she cannot control global markets, but she can control her own budget, one receipt at a time.",
    "articleZh": "当物价快速上涨时，普通消费者会改变自己的习惯。我的邻居莉娜会为她去的每家超市都留一张优惠券，而且在购买任何进口商品之前都会查一下汇率。在周末的集市上，她会用一袋苹果换一些新鲜鸡蛋，或者和朋友互换旧衣服，而不是买新的。她总是索要收据，而且为了她那家很小的网店，她会仔细归档每一张发票，因为税务局可能会要求提供凭证。\n\n政府也会做出反应。它们可能对含糖饮料征收额外费用，提高进口汽车的关税，或者在边境征收税费。当官员们实施这类规定时，家庭就会减少消费，花钱也更加谨慎。生活成本上升，日常开支增加，公司收入下降。如果这种情况持续下去，一个国家所生产的一切东西的总价值就会停止增长。\n\n这时经济学家就会开始使用一些严肃的词。衰退指的是经济连续萎缩六个月或更久。萧条则严重得多：需求长年疲弱、工厂关门、失业率高企。两者常常都始于一场银行业危机，也就是放贷方突然失去信心、不再提供贷款的时候。\n\n不过，普通人的选择依然重要。莉娜说，她无法控制全球市场，但她能管好自己的预算，一张收据一张收据地管。",
