    "id": "c18p3",
    "chapter": 18,
    "page": 3,
    "articleTitle": "The Patient General",
    "articleEn": "On the old battlefield, the loudest army was seldom the winning one. A wise general would {{deploy}} his troops on high ground and wait. He knew that one lucky {{strike}} of a sword decided nothing, while patience decided everything.\n\nThe enemy commander was {{aggressive}}. He hoped to {{beat}} the defenders in a single morning and {{seize}} the city gates. His men {{hit}} the wood with heavy logs, {{punched}} holes in it, {{chopped}} at the beams with axes, and {{stabbed}} their spears through every crack.\n\nInside, the defenders faced a {{dilemma}}: open the gates and fight, or stay in and slowly {{starve}}. Every choice had a {{pitfall}}. If they marched out, the cavalry would {{pursue}} them across open fields; if they stayed, {{famine}} would arrive before spring. The general outside understood this, so he simply chose to {{deprive}} the city of grain and water.\n\nTheir {{plight}} grew worse every week. Rich merchants became {{destitute}}, and families sold their furniture for a bowl of rice. Worse still, a {{conspiracy}} was forming: a few officers planned to open the north gate at night.\n\nYet the old general outside was careful never to {{offend}} the villagers nearby. He paid a fair price for their food, and in return they told him where the enemy camp was weakest. The greatest {{threat}} on a battlefield, he liked to say, is not a stronger arm, but a mind that stops thinking.",
    "articleZh": "在古代的战场上,喊声最大的一方很少是获胜的一方。一位明智的将军会把军队部署在高地上,然后等待。他明白,一次幸运的挥剑并不能决定什么,而耐心却能决定一切。\n\n敌方指挥官很好斗。他希望在一个上午之内就打败守军,夺取城门。他的士兵用沉重的木桩撞击城门,在门上凿出洞,用斧头砍那些横梁,还把长矛从每一道缝隙里刺进去。\n\n城内的守军面临一个两难困境:打开城门出去作战,还是留在城里慢慢饿死。每种选择都有陷阱。如果他们出城,骑兵就会在开阔的原野上追击他们;如果留下,饥荒会在春天之前到来。城外的将军明白这一点,所以他干脆选择断绝这座城的粮食和水源。\n\n他们的困境一周比一周严重。富商变得一文不值,家家户户为了一碗米而卖掉家具。更糟的是,一场阴谋正在形成:几名军官打算在夜里打开北门。\n\n不过,城外那位老将军始终小心,从不冒犯附近的村民。他为他们的粮食付了公道的价钱,作为回报,村民告诉他敌营最薄弱的地方在哪里。他常说,战场上最大的威胁不是更强壮的臂膀,而是一颗停止思考的头脑。",
