    "id": "c18p11",
    "chapter": 18,
    "page": 11,
    "articleTitle": "Training in the Desert",
    "articleEn": "Every summer, young {{soldiers}} travel to a desert camp to learn what a real battle feels like. On the first morning, a {{general}} tells them plainly, \"You {{owe}} your safety to the person standing beside you.\" Nobody laughs after that.\n\nThe day begins with a long {{patrol}} across burning sand. One team must {{guard}} a small water tower while the others try to take it. To stay hidden, the defenders paint their faces and cover their helmets with {{camouflage}} nets, so from a distance they look like dry bushes. Every {{obstacle}} on the route — a wall of tyres, a rope bridge, a narrow tunnel that forces them to {{burrow}} through the sand like animals — is designed to test patience, not only strength.\n\nLater, the attacking team sends a {{scout}} ahead to draw a map of the enemy camp. His job is not to {{spy}} on private secrets but to count vehicles and find a safe path. Meanwhile a {{veteran}} who fought twenty years ago watches quietly from a jeep. He says the hardest lesson is simple: speed matters far less than trust.\n\nBy sunset, everyone is covered in dust and too tired to argue. They have learned something no book can teach them. On a battlefield, it is the small habits — checking your water, watching your friend's back — that decide who goes home.",
    "articleZh": "每年夏天，年轻的士兵们都会前往沙漠营地，去体会真正的战斗是什么感觉。第一天早上，一位将军直截了当地告诉他们：\"你们的安全，要归功于站在你身旁的那个人。\"这句话之后，没有人再笑了。\n\n这一天以一次穿越滚烫沙地的长途巡逻开始。一支队伍必须守卫一座小水塔，其他人则设法把它夺过来。为了不被发现，防守方在脸上涂上颜料，并用伪装网盖住头盔，这样远远看去他们就像干枯的灌木。路线上的每一个障碍——轮胎堆成的墙、绳索桥、迫使他们像动物一样在沙中钻行的狭窄隧道——都是为了考验耐心，而不只是体力。\n\n后来，进攻方派出一名侦察兵先行前往，绘制敌方营地的地图。他的任务不是去窥探别人的私密，而是清点车辆、找出一条安全的路线。与此同时，一位二十年前参加过战斗的老兵坐在吉普车里静静地看着。他说最难学的一课其实很简单：速度远远不如信任重要。\n\n到了日落时分，所有人都满身尘土，累得连争吵都懒得开口。他们学到了书本无法教给他们的东西：在战场上，正是那些小小的习惯——检查自己的水壶、护好同伴的背后——决定了谁能回家。",
