    "id": "c19p1",
    "chapter": 19,
    "page": 1,
    "articleTitle": "Who We Are Expected to Be",
    "articleEn": "Every family records the slow change of social roles. My {{grandfather}} was a {{pioneer}} in our small town: he opened the first classes for {{female}} students, and neighbours called him a {{prophet}} because he insisted that girls would one day lead companies. Looking back, his little school was a {{herald}} of a much bigger change in the way people understood {{gender}}.\n\nIn his {{generation}}, the rules seemed fixed. A {{husband}} earned the money, his wife managed the home, and the eldest son was the {{heir}} to the family shop and the obvious {{successor}} to his father. The family {{surname}} travelled only through the men, and official forms asked for your {{sex}} as if that single word explained your whole future.\n\nMy own family looks different. I kept my surname; my {{spouse}} and I are a {{couple}} who share the cooking, the bills and the school runs. My {{sibling}} is {{gay}}, and he and his partner are raising two children, my {{nephew}} and {{niece}}. At first my {{father-in-law}} found all of this strange, but now he drives across the city for their birthday parties. Even my most traditional {{cousin}} admits that the family works better this way.\n\nRoles are not laws. They are habits, and habits can be rewritten, usually by someone brave enough to go first.",
    "articleZh": "每个家庭都记录着社会角色的缓慢变化。我的祖父是我们小镇上的先锋:他开办了第一批招收女性学生的班级,邻居们称他为先知,因为他坚信女孩总有一天会领导公司。回头看,他那所小学校是人们理解性别方式发生更大转变的先声。\n\n在他那一代人中,规则似乎是固定的。丈夫挣钱,妻子操持家务,长子是家中店铺的继承人,也是父亲理所当然的接班人。家族的姓氏只通过男性传下去,官方表格会询问你的性别,好像那一个词就能解释你的一生。\n\n我自己的家庭则不一样。我保留了自己的姓;我和配偶是一对共同承担做饭、账单和接送孩子上学的夫妻。我的兄弟是同性恋,他和伴侣正在养育两个孩子,也就是我的侄子和侄女。起初我的公公觉得这一切很奇怪,但现在他会开车穿过整座城市去参加他们的生日聚会。就连我最传统的表亲也承认,这个家庭这样运转得更好。\n\n角色不是法律。它们是习惯,而习惯是可以被改写的,通常是由某个勇敢地先走一步的人来改写。",
