    "id": "c20p6",
    "chapter": 20,
    "page": 6,
    "articleTitle": "The Quiet Boy in Our Class",
    "articleEn": "Last year our school faced a problem that still makes me {{marvel}} at how quickly people can change. A group of older students began to {{bully}} a quiet boy called Sam. At first they only wanted to {{annoy}} him: they hid his bag and laughed at his accent. Later the jokes became cruel enough to {{upset}} him badly. He would {{panic}} before every break, and some days the noise in the corridor seemed to {{terrify}} him so much that he chose to {{flee}} to the library.\n\nWhat {{astonished}} me most was the silence of the adults. Several teachers appeared to {{neglect}} the obvious signs, and one decided to {{disregard}} Sam's written complaint. Their {{ignorance}} of the facts was partly a matter of {{bias}}: the bullies were popular athletes, so nobody wanted to {{involve}} the head teacher. The {{irony}} was that our corridors were covered with posters about respect.\n\nIn the end Sam spoke to a new teacher who refused to treat his story with {{contempt}}. She did not {{deviate}} from the rules. She wrote everything down, interviewed witnesses, and passed the report upwards. Two students were {{expelled}}; the rest had to apologise in front of the class, which was probably the first real {{indignity}} they had ever felt.\n\nSam never looked for {{revenge}}. His quiet forgiveness seemed to {{bewilder}} the very boys who had hurt him, and it taught the rest of us more than any lesson could.",
    "articleZh": "去年我们学校发生了一件事，至今仍让我惊叹于人可以变得多快。一群高年级学生开始欺凌一个叫萨姆的安静男孩。起初他们只是想惹恼他：藏他的书包，嘲笑他的口音。后来那些玩笑变得残酷起来，足以让他非常难过。每次课间休息前他都会惊慌，有些日子走廊里的喧闹似乎让他极为恐惧，于是他选择逃到图书馆去。\n\n最让我震惊的是大人们的沉默。有几位老师似乎忽视了那些明显的迹象，还有一位决定对萨姆的书面投诉置之不理。他们对事实的无知，一部分源于偏见：那些欺凌者是受欢迎的运动员，所以没人愿意把校长牵扯进来。讽刺的是，我们的走廊里贴满了关于尊重的海报。\n\n最后，萨姆找了一位新老师谈话，她没有用轻蔑的态度对待他的经历。她没有偏离规定：她把一切都记录下来，询问了证人，并把报告递交上去。两名学生被开除了；其余的人必须在全班面前道歉，这大概是他们第一次真正感受到的屈辱。\n\n萨姆从未寻求报复。他平静的宽恕似乎让那些曾伤害他的男孩感到困惑，而这也教给我们其他人比任何一堂课都更多的东西。",
