    "id": "c21p15",
    "chapter": 21,
    "page": 15,
    "articleTitle": "Small Habits, Strong Mind",
    "articleEn": "A {{prominent}} doctor at our city clinic once gave me some {{sincere}} advice: caring for your body and mind is not one {{awesome}} act of willpower, but a thousand {{mundane}} choices repeated quietly.\n\nI used to be {{naive}} about this. I assumed that a {{promising}} fitness plan had to be {{arduous}} — an hour of {{strenuous}} exercise before dawn — and that anything gentler was {{childish}}. That idea was neither {{sane}} nor {{trustworthy}}.\n\nThe truth is calmer. A {{robust}} body grows out of {{temperate}} habits: moderate meals, regular sleep, a {{sturdy}} pair of walking shoes, and twenty honest minutes outdoors. Our minds are more {{delicate}} than we like to admit. People under constant pressure are {{liable}} to burn out, however {{tough}} they look from the outside. So learn to be {{tender}} with yourself on difficult days, and {{steadfast}} on ordinary ones.\n\nInterestingly, the most {{enterprising}} students I teach are also the most serious about rest. They plan their recovery the way they plan their projects, so when exams arrive their energy stays level while others collapse.\n\nHealth, then, is less about heroic effort than about patient repetition. Choose habits you can keep when you are bored, busy or sad. Small, dull, faithful actions slowly build a life that can carry weight.",
    "articleZh": "我们市里诊所的一位知名医生曾给过我一些诚挚的建议：照顾身体和心理，并不是某一次了不起的意志行为，而是一千次安静重复的平凡选择。\n\n以前我对这件事很天真。我以为一份有前途的健身计划必须是艰巨的——天亮前先做一小时费力的运动——而任何更温和的方式都是幼稚的。这种想法既不理智，也不可靠。\n\n真相要平静得多。强健的身体来自节制的习惯：饭吃得适量、睡眠规律、一双结实的步行鞋，以及每天二十分钟实实在在的户外活动。我们的心理比我们愿意承认的更脆弱。长期承受压力的人容易倦怠崩溃，无论他们从外表看起来多么坚强。所以要学会在难过的日子里对自己温柔，在平常的日子里保持坚定不移。\n\n有意思的是，我教过的最有进取心的学生，往往也最认真对待休息。他们像规划项目一样规划自己的恢复，所以考试来临时，他们的精力保持平稳，而别人却撑不住了。\n\n可见健康与其说靠英勇的努力，不如说靠耐心的重复。要选择那些在你无聊、忙碌或难过时也能坚持的习惯。微小、乏味却忠实的行动，会慢慢筑成一种承得住重量的生活。",
