    "chapter": 21,
    "page": 16,
    "articleTitle": "The Mountain Path Back to Health",
    "articleEn": "When I began training for a marathon, I followed a {{rigorous}} plan and a {{strict}} diet. For three months I made no exceptions. Then came a {{severe}} knee injury, and with it a kind of {{agony}} I had never felt before. The pain was physical, but the {{grief}} was mental: I had lost the identity I had built.\n\nMy physiotherapist and I developed a {{mutual}} understanding. She said recovery does not {{resemble}} a straight line; it looks more like a mountain path. Many of her patients had {{similar}} stories, and their feelings were {{familiar}} to her. She explained that healing needs an {{implicit}} agreement between body and mind: you must trust yourself even when no proof exists.\n\nShe asked me to walk on {{bare}} feet across grass, to stand under the {{naked}} sky, and to keep a {{private}} journal that nobody would ever read. At first I thought this was a {{fancy}} idea with no science behind it. But slowly my {{intuition}} came back, and I began to laugh in a {{spontaneous}} way again.\n\nThe biggest lesson was about control. I used to believe that perfect discipline would protect me, but that was an {{illusion}}, a comfortable {{imaginary}} shield. Health is partly {{fate}}, partly {{fortune}}, and partly the small choices we repeat every day. Now I train less, sleep more, and feel stronger than before.",
    "articleZh": "当我开始为马拉松训练时，我遵循着一套严格的训练计划和严苛的饮食。三个月里我没有破过一次例。后来我的膝盖受了重伤，随之而来的是一种我从未体会过的剧痛。疼痛是身体上的，但那种悲痛是心理上的：我失去了自己一手建立起来的身份认同。\n\n我和我的理疗师之间建立了相互的理解。她说康复并不像一条直线，它更像一条山路。她的许多病人都有类似的经历，他们的感受对她来说很熟悉。她解释说，痊愈需要身体与心灵之间一种含而不露的约定：即使没有任何证据，你也必须信任自己。\n\n她让我光着脚走过草地，站在空旷无遮的天空下，并写一本没有人会读到的私人日记。起初我觉得这是个花哨的想法，背后没有科学依据。但我的直觉慢慢回来了，我也重新开始自然而然地笑出声。\n\n最大的一课与控制有关。我曾经相信完美的自律能保护我，但那只是一种错觉，一面令人安心的、虚构的盾牌。健康一部分靠命运，一部分靠运气，还有一部分靠我们每天重复的那些小小选择。现在我训练得更少，睡得更多，却比从前更强壮。",
