    "id": "c21p17",
    "chapter": 21,
    "page": 17,
    "articleTitle": "Feelings and Your Health",
    "articleEn": "Doctors now agree that the mind and the body form one system. When we {{grieve}} after a loss, the whole body feels it: we sleep badly, eat poorly, and look {{miserable}} in the mirror. Long-term {{distress}} raises blood pressure and weakens the immune system, so pushing {{sadness}} away is not a healthy choice.\n\nThe real problem is that most of us try to hide dark feelings. On a {{gloomy}} winter morning, a student who has failed an exam may {{disappoint}} himself so deeply that he feels {{discouraged}} and stops trying. He calls himself {{wretched}}, finds every simple task {{frustrating}}, and quietly begins to {{mourn}} the future he had imagined for himself.\n\n{{Anger}} usually follows. Small things {{irritate}} us: a slow bus makes us {{furious}}, we {{frown}} at strangers, and a noisy neighbour becomes a {{nuisance}} we honestly {{hate}}. Some people carry that {{hatred}} for years, letting {{rage}} and {{disgust}} sit in the chest like a cold stone, until almost anything can {{vex}} them.\n\nPsychologists suggest a simple routine. First, name the feeling out loud. Second, move the body, because walking, swimming or even cleaning the kitchen calms the chemical storm inside us. Third, tell one person you trust. None of this removes the pain, but it stops the pain from controlling your health. Emotions are information, not enemies, and reading them well is a skill you can practise every day.",
    "articleZh": "医生现在一致认为，心理和身体是一个整体系统。当我们在失去之后感到悲痛时，全身都会有反应：睡不好，吃不下，照镜子时看上去一脸愁苦。长期的痛苦会让血压升高、免疫力下降，所以把悲伤强行推开并不是健康的做法。\n\n真正的问题在于，我们大多数人都试图掩藏阴暗的情绪。在一个阴沉的冬日早晨，一个考试没通过的学生可能会对自己极度失望，以至于灰心丧气、不再努力。他说自己很可怜、很糟糕，觉得每一件简单的事都令人沮丧，并暗暗地开始哀悼自己曾经设想过的未来。\n\n愤怒通常紧随其后。小事就会惹恼我们：公交车太慢就让人怒火中烧，我们对陌生人皱眉头，吵闹的邻居变成一个我们真心讨厌的麻烦。有些人把这种憎恨背了好多年，让盛怒和厌恶像一块冰冷的石头一样压在胸口，最后几乎任何事情都能让他们心烦意乱。\n\n心理学家建议一个简单的做法。第一，把这种感受大声说出名字。第二，让身体动起来，因为散步、游泳，甚至打扫厨房，都能平息我们体内的化学风暴。第三，告诉一个你信任的人。这些做法都无法消除痛苦，但它们能阻止痛苦控制你的健康。情绪是信息，不是敌人，而读懂情绪是一项你每天都可以练习的技能。",
