    "id": "c21p20",
    "chapter": 21,
    "page": 20,
    "articleTitle": "Learning to Read My Own Signals",
    "articleEn": "For months I would {{hesitate}} before admitting how exhausted I felt. My body's signals only {{confused}} me: headaches, broken sleep, a heart racing for no reason. Whenever I rested, I felt {{guilty}}, as though slowness were a crime. Asking for help seemed to {{embarrass}} me far more than the illness itself, and now I {{regret}} waiting so long.\n\nThere was nothing to {{repent}}: I had done nothing wrong. I was simply {{ashamed}} of being human. My own mind was {{sly}}, offering {{cunning}} excuses: 'You are just lazy.' It set {{arbitrary}} rules — no rest before midnight, no complaints ever — and I obeyed them without question. I would {{sigh}} at my desk, and sometimes, alone in the dark, I would {{weep}} for reasons I could not name.\n\nWhat changed was {{tolerance}}: not for pain, but for myself. I had been {{ignorant}} of a simple fact — nobody has to {{endure}} everything alone. My first counselling session felt {{awkward}}. My sentences were {{clumsy}}, and I apologised for wasting her time. She smiled and said that my worries were neither {{ridiculous}} nor {{stupid}}; they were signals I had been too {{careless}} to read.\n\nNow I sleep earlier, walk daily, and speak honestly. Health, I have learned, is not a reward for perfect behaviour. It is a conversation with your body that never really ends.",
    "articleZh": "有好几个月，我总是迟疑着不肯承认自己有多疲惫。身体发出的信号只让我更加困惑：头痛、睡不安稳、心跳无缘无故地加快。每当我休息，就会感到内疚，好像慢下来是一种罪过。开口求助带给我的窘迫，似乎远远超过疾病本身；如今我很后悔拖了那么久。\n\n其实我没有什么需要悔悟的：我并没有做错事，只是为自己身为普通人而感到羞愧。我自己的头脑很狡猾，会抛出一个个精明的借口：“你只是懒而已。”它还定下种种武断的规矩——半夜之前不许休息，永远不许抱怨——而我毫不质疑地服从。我常在书桌前叹气，有时独自待在黑暗里，会为说不清的理由哭泣。\n\n真正改变我的是宽容：不是对痛苦的宽容，而是对自己的宽容。我一直无知地忽略了一个简单的事实——没有人必须独自承受一切。第一次心理咨询让我觉得很尴尬，说出的话笨拙生硬，我还为浪费她的时间道歉。她笑着说，我的担忧既不荒唐也不愚蠢；它们是信号，只是我太粗心，一直没有读懂。\n\n现在我睡得更早，每天散步，也坦诚地表达自己。我明白了：健康不是表现完美的奖赏，而是一场与身体永不结束的对话。",
