    "id": "c21p21",
    "chapter": 21,
    "page": 21,
    "articleTitle": "Small Changes, Steady Health",
    "articleEn": "For years I was {{oblivious}} to how much my daily habits shaped my mood. I sat at a desk all day, felt {{sluggish}} by mid-afternoon, and reached for sugar whenever an {{impulse}} struck. I told myself I was simply {{slothful}} by nature, but that was just a {{stereotype}} I had accepted about myself.\n\nMy doctor was blunt. She said a healthy routine does not have to be {{rigid}}; it only has to be repeated. Instead of a punishing plan, she suggested three small changes: a short walk after lunch, water before coffee, and lights out by eleven. None of them were {{obtrusive}}, so they slipped into my day almost unnoticed.\n\nProgress was slow, because bad habits are {{stubborn}}. Some evenings I was {{greedy}} at dinner and ate far more than I needed. Other nights my phone kept me awake like a {{naughty}} child refusing to go to bed. Still, I stopped treating these slips as failures and simply started again the next morning.\n\nSix months on, my body feels lighter and my mind feels clearer. Mental and physical health, I have learned, are not two separate projects. When I move a little, sleep enough, and eat without guilt, my thoughts grow calmer as well. The change was never dramatic. It was patient, quiet, and completely ordinary, which is probably why it lasted.",
    "articleZh": "多年来，我一直没有意识到日常习惯对我的情绪影响有多大。我整天坐在办公桌前，到了下午就昏昏沉沉，只要一有冲动就去找甜食吃。我告诉自己，我天生就是懒散的人，但那只是我对自己所接受的一种刻板印象。\n\n我的医生说话很直接。她说健康的作息不必死板，只需要不断重复。她没有给我一个折磨人的计划，而是建议了三个小改变：午饭后散一会儿步、喝咖啡之前先喝水、十一点前关灯睡觉。这些都不算碍事，所以它们几乎不知不觉地融入了我的一天。\n\n进展很慢，因为坏习惯很顽固。有些晚上我吃饭时很贪吃，吃得远远超过身体的需要。还有些夜晚，手机让我睡不着，就像一个调皮的孩子赖着不肯上床。不过，我不再把这些小失误看作失败，只是在第二天早上重新开始。\n\n六个月后，我的身体感觉轻盈了，头脑也更清晰了。我明白了，心理健康和身体健康并不是两个彼此分开的项目。当我稍微活动、睡得足够、吃东西时不带负罪感，我的思绪也会变得更平静。这种改变从来都不惊天动地。它耐心、安静、完全平凡，而这大概正是它能坚持下来的原因。",
