    "id": "c22p1",
    "chapter": 22,
    "page": 1,
    "articleTitle": "A Museum of Time",
    "articleEn": "Our city museum has just opened an exhibition about how people measure time. It runs {{daily}} from {{dawn}} until {{midnight}}, so visitors can come before work or late at {{night}}. The exhibition opened on the {{anniversary}} of the museum's founding, exactly one {{century}} ago.\n\nThe first room covers the {{medieval}} period, when monks rang bells at {{midday}} and kept simple {{monthly}} calendars. A {{dozen}} old clocks stand in glass cases, and some are more than a thousand years old, from the previous {{millennium}}. Guides explain that each {{era}} invented its own tools, from water clocks to atomic ones.\n\nLater rooms bring you to the {{present}}. {{Contemporary}} artists have built screens that count every second: a {{million}} seconds pass in about eleven days, while a {{billion}} seconds take almost thirty-two years. Standing there, you feel very small.\n\nThe museum also uses the exhibition to teach planning. Staff publish a {{quarterly}} newsletter, hold {{yearly}} lectures for students, and their {{annual}} report explains how every pound is spent. Schools can borrow boxes of materials {{overnight}} and return them the next morning.\n\nWhatever your schedule looks like, there is a quiet moment for you somewhere in the building. Time, the guides like to say, belongs to everyone, and it never waits for anybody.",
    "articleZh": "我们市里的博物馆刚刚开办了一场关于人类如何测量时间的展览。它每天从黎明一直开放到午夜，所以参观者可以在上班前来，也可以在深夜来。展览开幕的那天正是博物馆成立的周年纪念日，距今整整一个世纪。\n\n第一个展厅讲述中世纪时期：那时僧侣在正午敲钟，并使用简单的月度日历。玻璃柜里陈列着十几座古老的钟表，有些已有一千多年历史，来自上一个千年。讲解员解释说，每个时代都发明了自己的工具，从水钟到原子钟。\n\n后面的展厅把你带到当下。当代艺术家制作了不断计数每一秒的屏幕：一百万秒大约在十一天内流逝，而十亿秒则要将近三十二年。站在那里，你会觉得自己非常渺小。\n\n博物馆还借这场展览来教人们做规划。工作人员出版季度通讯，为学生举办年度讲座，他们的年报会说明每一英镑是怎么花的。学校可以借走整箱教学材料，过夜后第二天早上归还。\n\n不管你的日程是什么样，这栋楼里总有一个属于你的安静时刻。讲解员爱说，时间属于每一个人，而它从不等待任何人。",
