    "id": "c22p2",
    "chapter": 22,
    "page": 2,
    "articleTitle": "Marks on a Map",
    "articleEn": "In {{modern}} life, our sense of time has changed. A {{recent}} survey showed that most students now keep their calendars {{up to date}} on their phones rather than on paper.\n\nTime management advice usually follows a simple order. {{First}} — and {{foremost}} — you write down every deadline. {{Secondly}}, you divide each big task into smaller steps. {{Finally}}, you decide when to {{finish}} each step.\n\nThe problem is that we expect everything to happen {{immediately}}. We send a message and want an {{instant}} reply. If nobody answers within a {{minute}}, we feel uneasy. But real progress takes more than a single {{moment}}.\n\nMy own habits are better now than in the {{preceding}} year. {{Prior}} to that, I studied only on the night before an exam. My {{former}} teacher called this a dangerous {{precedent}}, because one late night makes the next one easier to accept.\n\nSo I changed my system. {{Initially}}, I planned only the {{opening}} week of the term, because a whole year felt frightening. Then I added an {{intermediate}} review every Sunday, halfway between two tests. Now, even in the {{midst}} of a busy month, I know exactly what each day requires, and I rarely lose a single afternoon.\n\nDates are no longer my enemies. They are simply marks on a map that I have drawn myself.",
    "articleZh": "在现代生活中，我们对时间的感觉已经改变了。一项最近的调查显示，如今大多数学生把日程表放在手机上随时更新，而不是记在纸上。\n\n时间管理的建议通常遵循一个简单的顺序。首先，也是最重要的，把每一个截止日期都写下来。其次，把每项大任务分成更小的步骤。最后，决定什么时候完成每一个步骤。\n\n问题在于，我们期待一切都立刻发生。我们发出一条消息，就想要即时的回复。如果一分钟之内没人回应，我们就会感到不安。但真正的进步需要的不只是一瞬间。\n\n我现在的习惯比前一年好多了。在那之前，我只在考试前一晚才学习。我以前的老师说这是一个危险的先例，因为熬一次夜之后，下一次就更容易接受了。\n\n所以我改变了自己的方法。起初，我只规划学期开头的那一周，因为整整一年让我感到害怕。后来我在每个星期天加入了一次中间阶段的复习，正好在两次考试之间。现在，即使在忙碌月份的当中，我也清楚地知道每一天需要做什么，很少会浪费掉一个下午。\n\n日期不再是我的敌人。它们只是我自己画出的地图上的一些标记。",
